  "result": "The command now prints **27.06** (before the fix it printed 26.09), and all 7 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py` padded the cents digits on the wrong side. It used `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")`, so `\"12.5\"` became `\"12.05\"`, which is 1205 cents instead of 1250. Two of those come to $24.10, and adding 8.25% tax gives 26.09. Prices with two decimals like `12.50` were parsed correctly, which is why only one-decimal prices were wrong.\n\n**Fix:** I changed it to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`, so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"`. Now 2 \u00d7 $12.50 = $25.00, plus $2.06 tax, gives $27.06.\n\nI also added a regression test in `tests/test_money.py` that checks `to_cents` on `12`, `12.5`, `12.50`, `12.05` and `0.1`. None of the existing tests called `to_cents`, which is how the bug got through.",
